<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al Fadl</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al Fadl was an Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribe of the Syrian desert, descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayy">Tayy</a> and the Jarrahid family. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> its chiefs, notably the Al Muhanna line, held the office of amir al-arab, commander of the Bedouin, guarding desert routes and frontiers. Its power declined under Ottoman rule.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Al Fadl were one of the two main branches of the Banu Rabi'ah, a tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayy">Banu Tayy</a>; the other was the Al Mira. Fadl ibn Rabi'ah is noted in Muslim chronicles as an emir by 1107. With his father and brothers he commanded mounted auxiliaries for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughtekin">Tughtekin</a>, the Burid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and his Zengid successors against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>. Members of the tribe later claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmakids">Barmakids</a> or from al-Abbas, lineages that medieval and modern historians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> among them, have dismissed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Driven out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hauran">Hauran</a> by the Al Mira around the turn of the 13th century, the Al Fadl moved to the steppe around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, where the Ayyubid sultans paid them to keep the roads between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> safe, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adil">al-Adil</a> made Fadl's grandson Haditha amir al-ʿarab. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> they guarded the frontier with Ilkhanid Iraq and served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxilia">auxiliary troops</a> in return for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> and gifts, and from 1281 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> was one of their principal towns, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamiyah">Salamiyah</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1516, kept the office hereditary. In 1574 its emir was recognised with a plume-crowned turban, and his house became known as Al Abu Risha, leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawali">Mawali</a> confederation, which drove rival Al Fadl sheikhs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a>. In the 18th century the Fa'our branch moved on to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golan-heights">Golan Heights</a>, where it fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> and Kurdish groups and later Circassian settlers over pasture until a settlement in 1887.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>By the late 19th century the Fa'our lands in the Golan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hauran">Hauran</a> and the eastern Hula Valley were registered in the emir's name. Most tribesmen became his tenants, combining farming with herding, while the emir lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> as an absentee landlord. The 1948 Arab–Israeli War cost the tribe land in the Hula Valley, agrarian reforms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a> period took much of the emir's estate by 1958, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> of 1967 displaced the Al Fadl of the Golan entirely.</p><p>Most then settled in and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. The emir's role changed from benefactor to political representative: in 1964–1965 Fa'our al-Fa'our gained permission, through the Lebanese interior minister Kamal Jumblatt, for tribesmen in the Beqaa to buy land in the Anti-Lebanon Mountains. In the 1980s and early 1990s the Al Fadl were estimated at between 20,000 and 30,000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al Fadl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The family later regained influence among the Syrian Bedouin; Fadl ibn Rabi'a switched between Crusaders, Seljuks and Fatimids in the early 12th century, and his descendants led the <b>Al Fadl</b> tribe in Mamluk times.</blockquote><a href="/state/jarrahids">Jarrahids</a></li></ul></section>
